Once the project is set up, you can start by creating the 'Input' component. This component should handle the following requirements:

1. User input: The 'Input' component should have a property called 'onInput' which is a function that will be called whenever the user types something in the input field.

2. Dropdown menu: The 'Input' component should also have a property called 'items' which is an array of objects representing the options in the dropdown menu. Each object in the array should have two properties: 'value' and 'label'. The 'value' property should be the value that the dropdown menu should be submitted with when the user selects an option, while the 'label' property should be the text that should be displayed in the dropdown menu.

3. Validation: The 'Input' component should have a property called 'validation' which is an array of objects representing the validation rules for the input. Each object in the array should have two properties: 'value' and 'validationFunction'. The 'value' property should be the value that the validation rule should be applied to, while the 'validationFunction' property should be the function that should be called to perform the validation.

4. Handling edge cases and optimization: The 'Input' component should handle edge cases such as input values that are too long or invalid, and also optimize for a large number of options in the dropdown menu by using techniques such as virtualization and pagination.

Here is a basic implementation of the 'Input' component in TypeScript:

```typescript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

interface Props {
  onInput?: (value: string) => void;
  items?: { value: string, label: string }[];
  validation?: { value: string, validationFunction: (value: string) => boolean }[];
}

const Input: React.FC<Props> = ({ onInput, items, validation }) => {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('');
  const [inputError, setInputError] = useState(false);

  const handleInputChange = (event: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    setValue(event.target.value);
    setInputError(false);
  };

  const handleInputError = (validationFunction: (value: string) => boolean) => {
    setInputError(validationFunction(value));
  };

  const handleSubmit = () => {
    if (onInput) {
      onInput(value);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <input type="text" value={value} onChange={handleInputChange} />
      {inputError && <span style={{ color: 'red' }}>This input is invalid!</span>}
      {items && (
        <select>
          {items.map((item, index) => {
            return <option key={index} value={item.value}>{item.label}</option>;
          })}
        </select>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Input;
